# 11. Scan Types

Vulnerability scanners commonly provide different scan profiles.

Examples:

### Basic/General Scan

Looks for common vulnerabilities and configuration problems.

### Credentialed Scan

The scanner is provided authorized credentials and can inspect the system more deeply.

### Uncredentialed Scan

The scanner assesses the target without logging in.

### Web Application Scan

Focuses on web application security issues.

### Network Scan

Focuses on network hosts, services, configurations, and vulnerabilities.

---

# 12. Credentialed vs Uncredentialed Scanning

## Uncredentialed Scan

```text
Scanner
   ↓
Network
   ↓
Publicly accessible information
```

The scanner does not authenticate to the target.

It may have limited visibility.

---

## Credentialed Scan

```text
Scanner
   ↓
Authentication
   ↓
Target System
   ↓
Internal Checks
```

The scanner can inspect more information.

### Advantages

- Better visibility
- More accurate patch detection
- More detailed configuration checks
- Better identification of installed software

### Memory

> **Credentialed = Inside View**

> **Uncredentialed = Outside View**

---

# 13. Why Credentialed Scans Can Be Better

Suppose a scanner sees:

```text
Service version: Unknown
```

An uncredentialed scan may not be able to determine whether a patch is installed.

A properly configured credentialed scan may inspect:

- Installed software
- Patch levels
- Configuration
- Local security settings

Therefore:

> **Credentialed scanning often provides deeper visibility.**

